  "notes": "South wins the opening spade lead and must play on clubs to come to 9 tricks. As South has only 1 more spade stopper, he cannot afford to lose the lead more than once in clubs.\n\nSouth should play a club towards dummy at trick 2. When West follows low, South must decide which club to play from North. The ♣A should not be considered. The choice is between the ♣Q and the ♣10 (or any other low card in clubs).\n\nIf clubs are 3-3, the ♣Q will work when West has only the ♣K. The ♣10 will work when West has only the ♣J. These 2 chances are the same. If both club honours are together South's choice is irrelevant.\n\nIf East has 4 clubs, again it doesn't matter what South does. If West has 4 clubs to the ♣J, South cannot succeed. If West has 4 clubs to the ♣K, the ♣Q must be played from dummy. The ♣Q is also a winner if East has a singleton ♣J.\n\nSince playing the ♣Q picks up more holdings, South should, therefore, play the ♣Q at trick 2.",
